# File spec for ./update.rhai — the scan-and-rewrite lane the Rust side of
# /agent/src/package/update.sdd evaluates: `import("update")` then `update::run(args)`.
# Script semantics only: the clap surface, the `args` JSON, the resolver-dir order and the
# exit-2 trap are that spec's; the yaml/semver/registry/fs cores are vynil-core 0.7.7's and
# the @YamlDoc indexer legs /common/src/yamlhandler.sdd's. Calibrated on ./build.sdd and
# ./../lib/package_yaml.sdd. Behaviour below was exercised end-to-end over the real
# @YamlDoc and locked rhai with a stub registry (see Tasks).

Spec: PackagesUpdate

Platform: Rhai script (vynil-core 0.7.7 engine / locked rhai 1.25.1)

Purpose:
  Own the whole script side of `agent package update`: list every `images` entry's
    registry tags, keep the semver-parsable ones, bump each image's string `tag` to the
    newest, roll untagged (or non-string-tagged) images into one app-wide candidate that
    bumps `metadata.app_version` when it outranks it, and re-emission-write
    `{source}/package.yaml` in place at most once per run; everything sits between the
    optional `update_pre` (first) and `update_post` (last) hook calls. No k8s contact,
    no staging, no push, no Handlebars.
  Complete file structure: one top-level line `import "package_yaml" as pck;` then one
    @fn @run and nothing else — importing the module evaluates the @pck import and defines
    @run, with no other top-level effect; @run takes `args`.

Exposes:
  @run(args) — the module's sole @fn, called by the Rust snippet; its return value is
    discarded upstream, and success is completion (return-never-inspected verdict).
  Import contract: `import "update" as update` plus this file's own `package_yaml` import
    and its @import_run hook names `update_pre` / `update_post` all resolve over the Rust
    lane's four resolver dirs — `{source}/scripts`, `{config_dir}` (default `.` = process
    cwd), `{script_dir}/packages`, `{script_dir}/lib` — so the first entry (or the default
    cwd) shadows this module OR @pck OR either hook by name. A shadowed `package_yaml`
    replaces the gate; the bare @pck import lives at module-evaluation time, so
    module-not-found aborts before @run is ever reached — unlike the hooks, whose
    absence is the core @import_run shim's debug-log skip.

Accepts:
  @args — the injected scope map; the ONLY key read is `source`, used twice as the
    `+`-concatenated manifest path `{source}/package.yaml` for @file_read and @file_write;
    `script_dir`/`config_dir` never appear here, and neither is validated — an absent
    `source` key yields @() and the `+` concat raises.
  The manifest is taken as text → @yaml_decode_ordered into an ordered @YamlDoc map type
    (registered name `map`), never @yaml_decode; decode failures are plain raises.
  Expected shape: `metadata.app_version` (read only when `metadata` is a map), `images` as
    a map of entries each with `registry`, `repository` and optionally a string `tag`.
    Nothing is shape-checked first — see Raises for what malformed entries do.

Returns:
  `()` — side effects only. ZERO @print anywhere (unlike ./build.rhai): the only
    user-visible outputs are the per-image @log_info `Scanning ${registry}/${repository}`
    line emitted BEFORE any registry contact and the @log_warn lines below.

Raises:
  One scripted @throw in the file, verbatim with runtime interpolation:
    `Tag found for ${yaml["images"][k]["registry"]}/${yaml["images"][k]["repository"]} (${newt}) doesn't match the tag found for other images (${found})` —
    raised from the second untagged image whose newest candidate differs from the
    established one, mid-loop, before any write.
  @pck::validate's eight throw texts ride the tag-only pass and the fallback pass
    (./../lib/package_yaml.sdd owns them).
  Engine raises, uncaught — every one aborts the run as the Rust side's
    `Rhai script error:` exit-2 lane (/agent/src/package/update.sdd via its mod): @file_read stdio on a
    missing/unreadable `{source}/package.yaml`; the `YamlError:`-prefixed decode failure;
    @new_registry or @list_tags core errors (real network contact, per-image, no retry, no
    per-image catch); @list_tags or
    @new_registry called with @() from an `images` entry that is a scalar or lacks
    `registry`/`repository` (function-not-found on the @() argument aborts the whole run);
    @file_write stdio on the tag-only pass or on the fallback's retry; a
    `update_pre`/`update_post` module whose body throws (the shim bare-rethrows
    non-module/function errors); a failing @semver_from on a candidate comparison outside
    the two guarded legs.
  Caught, never propagating: non-semver LISTED tags (the filter leg's
    `try{let x = semver_from(v);true}catch(e){false}` — v-prefixed counts as parsable);
    a non-semver CURRENT string tag → @log_warn of the raw error when @type_of(@e) is
    `string`, else of @json_encode(@e), old text kept; the whole candidate leg —
    @semver_from(@app_version) refusing an unparseable or non-string `app_version`, AND a
    failed @file_write inside it (a candidate write that errors with `changed` unset is
    silently swallowed: run completes, nothing persists; with `changed` set the fallback
    @file_write failure is NOT caught and fails the run).

Handles:
  `images` absent or not a map → zero scanning, zero writing; both hooks still run
    (@update_post lives outside the `images` gate — it runs even on a manifest with no
    `images`).
  Per entry, in `yaml["images"].keys()` insertion order: log the Scanning line,
    `new_registry(registry, "", "")` → @list_tags(repository) → keep semver-parsable tags
    → @semver_from each → sort descending → `tags[0]` = newest; empty answer → `continue`
    (entry skipped without warning).
  String `tag` leg: newest `>` current parses OK → assign the bump text and set `changed`;
    current tag unparseable → warn, keep, `changed` NOT set; current newer or equal →
    silent no-op.
  Non-string `tag` leg (missing, int, map — anything not `type_of` `string`) participates
    ONLY when `app_version != ()`: first entry seeds the candidate, a differing candidate
    throws the Tag-found text, equal candidate is a no-op; with `app_version` `()` the
    entry is inert (no candidate is even recorded).
  Post-loop write discipline, at most one successful @file_write per run — the three
    arms, fully proven:
      candidate newer — `new_version` set, parse-compare succeeds, `new_version > old_v`:
      assign `metadata.app_version` to the `to_string()` text and @file_write the
      @yaml_encode_ordered re-emission with NO @pck::validate gate; same-run tag bumps are
      already in the map and ride along un-gated.
      catch fallback — `app_version` unparseable/non-string OR the candidate write failed:
      run @pck::validate and rewrite ONLY when `changed`; otherwise write nothing and
      finish clean.
      tag-only — no candidate and `changed`: @pck::validate then @file_write.
  Every other combination writes NOTHING: a clean or already-newest manifest keeps its
    bytes (no normalization rewrite, proven byte-identical), a candidate equal to
    `app_version` discards same-run `changed` tag bumps forever (Tasks), an untagged entry
    without `app_version` contributes nothing.
  @pck::validate runs over the @YamlDoc on the gated passes and COMPLETES: rhai dot access
    on the custom type falls back to the registered `[]` indexer pair, so the header
    injections, `requirements: []` default and @properties_improve rewrites verifiably land
    in the rewritten file alongside the bumps (settles the ./../lib/package_yaml.sdd
    dot-access question; the misspelled-default decision stays there).
  Ordering gives no atomicity: a registry that dies mid-loop aborts before the (single)
    write so one dead `repository` blocks every other image's bump; @update_post runs AFTER
    the write, so a throwing post hook leaves an updated manifest plus a failed run.

Must:
  Persist through exactly two channel facts — the chained map writes
    `yaml["images"][k]["tag"] = new_tag.to_string()` and
    `yaml["metadata"]["app_version"] = new_version.to_string()` DO reach the document, and
    the ONLY persistence is whole-file `@file_write({source}/package.yaml,
    @yaml_encode_ordered(yaml))` — never line-level, never a partial patch. Chained writes
    propagate because @YamlDoc registers BOTH indexer legs and locked rhai's
    index/dot-chain assign re-sets each hop's modified temp child through its parent's
    setter — an `idx_get` clone is not the end of the story (this settles the
    /agent/src/package/update.sdd chained-write `[?]` against execution; see Tasks).
  Re-emit with @yaml_encode_ordered — rust-yaml RoundTrip dump (insertion order preserved,
    comments-preserved config); the re-emission's reshaping hazards belong to
    /common/src/yamlhandler.sdd.
  Compare versions ONLY through the @Semver registered ops
    (@semver_from / `>` / `==` / `!=` / @to_string) — @Semver ordering is the DERIVED
    lexicographic order of `(InnerVersion, use_v)`, so at equal versions a `v`-prefixed
    tag is strictly GREATER than the bare one: manifest tag `8.0.0` against listed
    `v8.0.0` bumps (gaining the `v`), and untagged images answering `v2.0.0` vs `2.0.0`
    count as divergent candidates and throw. @to_string round-trips the `v` verbatim into
    the written text.
  Sort with the exact descending comparator `|a,b| if a==b {0} else if a<b {1} else {-1}`
    and take `tags[0]`; keep the @filter-before-@map order (filter re-parses via
    @semver_from inside the try, @map parses again — each surviving tag parses twice).
  Read `app_version` exactly once, up front, as `if type_of(yaml["metadata"]) == "map"
    {yaml["metadata"]["app_version"]} else {()}` — `()` (absent metadata, absent key,
    non-map metadata) turns the whole candidate machinery off.
  Gate @pck::validate as exactly `yaml = @pck::validate(yaml)` (rebound from the return)
    on the tag-only and fallback passes, never on the candidate-newer pass.
  Query registries anonymously and unguarded: `@new_registry(registry, "", "")` — empty
    credentials, core @RegistryAuth anonymous posture, 100-tag single-page @list_tags cap
    (no continuation), NO pull-secret/@get_auth_from use anywhere; a `>100`-tag repository
    can have its true newest outside the returned page.
  Keep @import_run hook sites: `@import_run("update_pre", args)` as @run's FIRST
    statement, `@import_run("update_post", args)` after the write decision, reached
    whenever @run reaches its end normally — including no-scan and no-write runs; no
    shipped `update_pre`/`update_post` exists in the tree, so today both legs are the
    core shim's `log_debug No ${name} module, skipping.`
  Stay pure-script: the consumed engine surface is exactly @file_read/@file_write,
    @yaml_decode_ordered/@yaml_encode_ordered, @log_info/@log_warn (+ the shim's
    @log_debug), @json_encode, @semver_from/@Semver/@to_string, @new_registry/@list_tags,
    @import_run, and @type_of/array @filter/@map/@sort/@len/@keys — no k8s names, no hbs,
    no @print, no other lib imports.

Must not:
  Write any file other than `{source}/package.yaml`, nor touch manifest fields beyond
    `images.*.tag` and `metadata.app_version` — @pck::validate's header/requirements/options
    edits ride the gated write as its side effect only.
  Authenticate, read credentials, retry a failed registry call, or catch a per-image
    network failure into a continue.

Depends on:
  ./../lib/package_yaml.rhai (./../lib/package_yaml.sdd) — imported as `pck`; @validate is
    the tag-only/fallback gate with its throws and warnings.
  /agent/src/package/update.sdd — the caller and every surrounding Rust fact (clap, args
    JSON, resolver dirs, exit ladder); the write-discipline decisions (candidate-discard
    arm, gate asymmetry, shadow, docs-hook) are owned there — pointer, not fork.
  /common/src/yamlhandler.sdd — @YamlDoc + the registered indexer pair + the ordered
    encode/decode this lane's persistence rides on; carries the stale `idx_get`-clone
    never-writes-through wording this spec's execution contradicts (Tasks).
  vynil-core 0.7.7 — @Registry anonymous auth + 100-tag page, @Semver parse/compare/
    @to_string, `fs` @file_read/@file_write, @json_encode, the @import_run/@assert common
    shims, tracing-backed @log_*; locked rhai 1.25.1's chain re-set propagation and
    dot→indexer fallback are the mechanics the persistence posture relies on.
  /common/src/rhaihandler.sdd — @Script::new assembly supplying this file's engine names.

References:
  /agent/tests/rhai_build.rs — the mock lane proves the absence: it never imports
    `update`, and mock @list_tags answers `[]` only, so the scan legs are untestable
    through it as-is.
  ./build.sdd — sibling script spec for the shared conventions (@security_filter absence,
    the v-prefix drift pairing, the listing-posture contrast: build catches its listing
    failure, update does not).

Tasks:
  [ ] Map the Scenarios below into tests — `agent/tests/` has ZERO coverage of this module
      today; the proof harness that settled the persistence questions (real @YamlDoc,
      locked rhai, stub @list_tags per repository against this exact file) belongs ported;
      ./../../tests/rhai_build.rs must first gain a stub/mock registry that answers tags.
  [?] Chained-write `[?]` of /agent/src/package/update.sdd — SETTLED BY EXECUTION here:
      bumps land and persist via the two frozen passes; a clean run rewrites nothing; both
      write scenarios hold. The surviving decision is documentary: move
      /common/src/yamlhandler.sdd's `idx_get`-clone never-writes-through wording (and the
      Rust spec's "rests on the chained-write decision" scenario lines) onto the
      registered-indexer-pair write-back truth. Owners: those two specs, not this file.
  [?] Dot-access-on-@YamlDoc `[?]` of ./../lib/package_yaml.sdd — SETTLED BY EXECUTION
      here: the tag-only pass reaches @validate and completes (property access falls back
      to the indexer pair; injections observably persist). Carry the wording change there;
      the misspelled-default decision stays in /agent/src/package/update.sdd.
  [?] Candidate-not-newer arm is a permanent discard (proven): candidate equal to
      `app_version` while string tags bumped → no pass writes, forever, silently. Likely
      lane bug; paired with /agent/src/package/update.sdd's same-named `[?]` — decide once.
  [?] Candidate-newer pass persists WITHOUT @pck::validate (proven: a manifest bumped this
      way still has no `apiVersion`/`kind`/`requirements` defaults while the same file's
      tag-only run injects them) — gate asymmetry decided once in
      /agent/src/package/update.sdd.
  [?] Candidate write failure with `changed` unset is swallowed: the run exits success
      with nothing persisted (the catch's retry only fires on `changed`). Decide whether a
      failed @file_write must always abort.
  [?] `v`-prefix artefacts of @Semver's derived ordering (bump `8.0.0`→`v8.0.0`;
      `v2.0.0` vs `2.0.0` candidates throw) — same family as ./build.sdd's `v`-prefix
      drift `[?]`; normalize-or-freeze must be decided for both lanes together.
  [?] @list_tags one-page-100 cap can miss an untagged or tagged image's true newest on
      busy repositories (core-owned posture, already frozen in ./build.sdd) — confirm
      acceptance for the update lane or fix in vynil-core.
  [?] `update_pre`/`update_post` ship nowhere (/docs/cli.md advertises the post hook;
      only shadow trees can supply them) — decision owned by
      /agent/src/package/update.sdd; this spec only freezes the call sites.

Done when:
  Every Scenario carries a mapped passing test or a written exemption, the two
    execution-settled wording moves are recorded in the specs they belong to, the `[?]`s
    are decided, and `cargo test -p agent` with clippy, `cargo +nightly fmt --check` and
    `specdd lint` stay green.

Scenario: string tag bump persists and is idempotent
  Given a fully authored manifest with image tag `8.0.35` and listed tags
    `8.0.35`, `latest`, `8.0.40`
  When @run completes
  Then the rewritten `{source}/package.yaml` carries tag `8.0.40` and no `8.0.35`
  And an immediate second @run leaves the file byte-identical
  Proven by the proof harness; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: tag-only pass gates through validate on @YamlDoc
  Given a manifest with no `apiVersion`, no `kind`, no `requirements`, one `options`
    property carrying only a `default`, and a bumpable string tag
  When @run completes
  Then the single write carries the bumped tag AND the injected
    `vinyl.solidite.fr/v1beta1`/`Package`/`requirements: []` AND the
    @properties_improve'd `type: string` — all through dot-assignment onto a @YamlDoc
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: candidate-newer writes the app_version un-gated
  Given untagged images, `app_version: 1.0.0`, newest uniform candidate `2.0.0`, manifest
    with no headers
  When @run completes
  Then `app_version: 2.0.0` is persisted and NO `apiVersion`-style defaults were injected
  And the second run writes nothing
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: candidate not newer discards collected tag work
  Given `app_version: 2.0.0`, one untagged image newest `2.0.0`, and another image whose
    string tag bumps `1.0.0`→`1.5.0`
  When @run completes
  Then it exits success and the file still carries `tag: 1.0.0` — the `changed` bump is
    never written while a candidate stands (equal is never >)
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: unparseable app_version falls back to the gated write
  Given `app_version: abc`, an untagged image seeding a candidate, and a bumpable string
    tag
  When @run reaches the candidate comparison
  Then @semver_from throws into the catch and the fallback validates and writes: bumped tag
    plus header defaults persist, `abc` survives unchanged
  And with `changed` unset the same catch writes nothing and the run ends clean
  Proven (first leg); no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: divergent v-sensitive candidates throw before any write
  Given two untagged images (or more, with `app_version` set) whose newest candidates
    differ — `2.0.0` then `3.0.0`, or `v2.0.0` then `2.0.0` equally
  When the loop reaches the second image
  Then the run fails with `Tag found for <registry>/<repository> (<newt>) doesn't match
    the tag found for other images (<found>)` and the manifest is untouched
  Proven (first pair); no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: non-semver current tag warns and retires
  Given the only image tagged `latest`, registry offering `1.0.0`/`2.0.0`
  When @run completes
  Then the run reports success, @log_warn received the failure text, `latest` stays and —
    because `changed` was never set and no candidate exists — the file is byte-untouched
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: untagged entry inert without app_version
  Given an untagged image and a metadata map WITHOUT `app_version`
  Then @run succeeds, writing nothing: the candidate machinery is switched off by `()`
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: dead registry blocks the whole scan
  Given two bumpable images where the SECOND repository's @list_tags raises
  Then the run fails and NEITHER bump was persisted — the loop's later failure kills the
    earlier in-memory work because the single write sits after the loop
  Proven; no mapped repo test exists.

Scenario: post hook runs after the write and is not atomic with it
  Given a shadow `update_post` (resolvable via the resolver dirs) on a manifest due one
    tag bump
  Then the hook executes after the rewrite (sentinel side effect observable), and a
    THROWING `update_post` turns the run red while the bumped manifest lies on disk
  Proven both legs; no mapped repo test exists.

Example:
  scan line per image, before contact: `Scanning docker.io/library/mysql`
  args consumed: only `source` — manifest path is `source + "/package.yaml"`, doubled
    slash accepted for a trailing-separator `--source`
  comparator as written: `|a,b| if a==b {0} else if a<b {1} else {-1}` — descending by
    @Semver order, `tags[0]` newest
  derived-order artefact: manifest `tag: 8.0.0` with listed `v8.0.0` bumps the `v` prefix ON.
